    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Citizenship Education in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>Citizenship is the status of being a legal member of a particular country. When you're a citizen, you have certain rights and privileges in that country, and you also have duties to help and support the country.</p>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Types of Citizenship</strong></h2>
<p>There are several ways a person can become a citizen of a country:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>By Birth</strong>: If you're born in a country or born to parents who are citizens of that country, you automatically become a citizen there. For example, if you're born in Nigeria to Nigerian parents, you are a Nigerian citizen.</li>
<li><strong>By Registration</strong>: This is often through marriage. For instance, if a person from one country marries a citizen of another country, they can register to become a citizen of their spouse's country.</li>
<li><strong>By Descent</strong>: If your parents are citizens of a country but you are born abroad, you can claim citizenship through them.</li>
<li><strong>By Naturalization</strong>: This is for foreigners who have lived in a country for a long time and want to become citizens. They need to show they are of good character and want to continue living in that country.</li>
<li><strong>By Honorary</strong>: Sometimes, a country might give citizenship to a foreigner who has done something very special or helpful for that country.</li>
</ol>
<h2><strong>Rights and Duties of Citizens</strong></h2>
<p>Being a citizen is not just about enjoying the benefits and rights provided by the country; it also means fulfilling certain responsibilities.</p>
<p><strong>Rights of Citizens</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Right to Life</strong>: Every citizen has the fundamental right to live.</li>
<li><strong>Freedom of Movement</strong>: You can travel anywhere within your country.</li>
<li><strong>Freedom of Expression</strong>: You can speak your mind, write, and publish freely.</li>
<li><strong>Right to Vote</strong>: You can vote in elections and even run for public office.</li>
<li><strong>Right to Education and Property</strong>: You can get an education and own property.</li>
<li><strong>Right to Fair Hearing and Privacy</strong>: You are entitled to a fair trial and have a right to private life.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Duties of Citizens</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Obey the Law</strong>: Follow the laws of the country.</li>
<li><strong>Pay Taxes</strong>: Contribute to the development of the country through taxes.</li>
<li><strong>Respect National Symbols</strong>: Honor the symbols like the flag and the national anthem.</li>
<li><strong>Defend the Country</strong>: Protect the country if needed.</li>
<li><strong>Vote</strong>: Participate in elections to choose leaders.</li>
<li><strong>Protect Public Property</strong>: Take care of things that belong to the public.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Practice Questions</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>What is one right and one duty of a citizen in Nigeria?</li>
<li>Explain how someone can become a citizen by naturalization.</li>
<li>Why is it important for citizens to pay taxes?</li>
<li>What does the right to freedom of expression allow you to do?</li>
<li>How can you become a citizen by registration?</li>
</ol>`
