    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>A<strong> government</strong> is a group of people who control and make decisions for a country or state. They create laws, enforce them, and ensure the smooth running of the country.</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Types of Government</strong></h1>
<p>Here are some common types of government:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Anarchy</strong>:

	<ul>
<li>Anarchy means the absence of government. It is a belief system that rejects governmental authority in favor of self-governing or community consensus. Anarchy often leads to chaos and disorder.</li>
<li>Example: In theory, an anarchist society has no rulers and everyone governs themselves.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Oligarchy</strong>:
	<ul>
<li>An oligarchy is a government ruled by a few individuals who have power and control.</li>
<li>Example: Russia, Turkey.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Monarchy</strong>:
	<ul>
<li>A monarchy is a government ruled by a king or queen. This position is usually inherited.</li>
<li>Example: United Kingdom, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, Spain.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Democracy</strong>:
	<ul>
<li>Democracy is a government of the people, by the people, and for the people. Citizens elect their leaders.</li>
<li>Example: Nigeria, United States of America, Ghana.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Military Government</strong>:
	<ul>
<li>In a military government, the armed forces hold the supreme power. Nigeria experienced military rule from 1966-1978 and 1983-1999.</li>
<li>Example: Major General Aguiyi Ironsi was Nigeria's first military head of state.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Aristocracy</strong>:
	<ul>
<li>An aristocracy is a government in which wealthy nobles hold power. This status is typically hereditary.</li>
<li>Example: Historically, many European countries.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Differences Between Monarchy and Democracy</p>
<table border="1" style="width:400px">
<thead>
<tr>
<th><strong>Monarchy Government</strong></th>
<th><strong>Democratic Government</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>No fixed tenure. Power is passed from parents to children.</td>
<td>Fixed tenure, usually four or five years.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Rules according to traditions and customs.</td>
<td>Rules according to the constitution.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Only people from a specific family can rule.</td>
<td>Leaders are elected by the people through elections.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>The king or queen stays in power until death or abdication.</td>
<td>Government can be changed through elections.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>It is the oldest form of government.</td>
<td>It is a modern form of government.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Constituted Authority</strong></h1>
<p><strong>Constituted authority</strong> refers to the different forms of leadership appointed or elected for the smooth running of society. These leaders can be found in schools, churches, hospitals, and the government.</p>
<p>Types of Constituted Authority</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Organizational</strong>: Bank managers, chief executive officers, headmasters, and principals.</li>
<li><strong>Traditional</strong>: Traditional rulers like Obas, emirs, Obongs, and Olus.</li>
<li><strong>Political</strong>: Presidents, governors, ministers, councilors, and senators.</li>
<li><strong>Religious</strong>: Priests, pastors, bishops, and imams.</li>
</ol>
<p>Functions of Constituted Authority</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Maintaining Law and Order</strong>: Ensuring laws are followed.</li>
<li><strong>Providing Social Amenities</strong>: Offering public services like water, electricity, and education.</li>
<li><strong>Protecting Lives and Property</strong>: Ensuring citizens' safety.</li>
<li><strong>Protecting Political Rights</strong>: Upholding citizens' rights.</li>
<li><strong>Promoting Economic Development</strong>: Encouraging growth and prosperity.</li>
<li><strong>Providing Employment</strong>: Creating job opportunities.</li>
<li><strong>Interpreting Holy Books</strong>: Guiding people in their faith.</li>
<li><strong>Defending the Country</strong>: Protecting against internal and external threats.</li>
</ol>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Good Governance</strong></h1>
<p><strong>Governance</strong> is how a country or organization is managed or controlled.</p>
<p><strong>Good governance</strong> means managing a country’s resources well to ensure growth and development. It involves transparency, respect for citizens' rights, and promoting peace and development.</p>
<p><strong>Importance of Good Governance</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Peace and Development</strong>: Ensures the country is peaceful and developing.</li>
<li><strong>Respect for Rights</strong>: Protects citizens' rights.</li>
<li><strong>Transparency</strong>: Builds trust in leaders.</li>
<li><strong>Securing the Future</strong>: Ensures a better future for the youth.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Practice Questions</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>What is a government?</li>
<li>Name two types of government and explain them.</li>
<li>What is the difference between a monarchy and a democracy?</li>
<li>List two functions of constituted authority.</li>
<li>Why is good governance important?</li>
</ol>`
